  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with a broad upper ridge still extending \nfrom the Big Horn Mountains and Black Hills to over the Great \nPlains. Temperatures have cooled down a bit and heat index values \nhave fallen below Heat Advisory Criteria. Therefore, the Heat \nAdvisory and Extreme Heat Warning have been allowed to expire for \nthe entire CWA. With the placement of the ridge, wildfire smoke is \nexpected to impact the region. We are not currently expecting any \nmajor impacts to visibility; however, some hazy skies and a faint \nsmell of smoke may be possible overnight into Tuesday morning. \n\nCloud cover increases overnight, along with our chances for a few \nshowers/storms. A weak shortwave trough will move into the area \nlater tonight bringing a chance (20%) for a few isolated \nthunderstorms across our area. Any storms that are able to make it \ninto our area may have the potential of producing some strong wind \ngusts. \n\nCloud cover sticks around Tuesday morning, decreasing into the \nafternoon. A few models try to bring in some isolated showers/storms \nin the morning, with those chances diminishing heading into the \nafternoon. We will finally see some relief from the dangerous heat \nTuesday afternoon with highs only reaching up into the upper 80s to \nlow 90s. A few models try to bring in some chances (20%) for a few \nshowers/storms Tuesday afternoon. Wednesday, the ridge shifts east \nslightly with high pressure over New Mexico, eastern Colorado and \nwestern Kansas. Expect another warm day with highs once again \ntopping out in the mid-80s to low 90s for most areas. \n\nExpect a pattern shift on Thursday as low pressure develops to our \nwest. This system will move across Nebraska during the day, tapping \ninto warm, gulf moisture and bringing it north into the region. \nThere will be chances for showers and storms during the day and into \nthe evening hours. Models are showing precipitable water values of \nat least an inch with several showing potential for 1.5\" or more. \nWe're still a few days away from the event, but if this trend \ncontinues and thunderstorms develop, we may need to monitor for \nlocalized flooding potential.\n\nShower/thunderstorm chances continue into Friday, tapering off west \nto east as the system moves into central Iowa and Minnesota. Enjoy \nsome cooler temperatures as highs stay in the 80s across the region \nand lows Saturday morning fall into the low to mid-60s. The cooler \nair sticks around through the weekend with highs mainly in the 80s \nand lows in the 60s.",
